<h2>Ask how the supplier stocks the style</h2>
<p>A catalogue photo of a coordinated outfit tells you little about the supplier's inventory. Ask for the set code and, if available, both component codes. “Mix sizes” might mean ordering several standard sizes in one order, or packing an S top with M leggings inside the same bag. Use those exact words in your enquiry to avoid receiving a quotation for the wrong arrangement.</p>
<h2>Prepare a pairing matrix instead of one total</h2>
<p>Illustrative request: 12 S/S sets, 18 S/M sets and 20 M/M sets in one colour. This totals 50 sets: 30 S tops, 20 M tops, 12 S bottoms and 38 M bottoms. A stock report showing 50 tops and 50 bottoms can still fail the plan if the supplier has only 25 M bottoms. This example is a calculation, not an advertised minimum or inventory statement.</p>
<p>If you buy separates, send two line items with separate size quantities and decide which combinations you will assemble or list yourself. If you want the factory to pack the pairs, give it the top size, bottom size and quantity per pairing, plus the label text on the outside. Decide how a split-size pair will be identified during receiving and how returns of one component will be handled in your own store.</p>
<h2>Approve a colour pair, not just two independent samples</h2>
<p>Compare the top and bottom side by side in the agreed light. Similar colour names do not prove a match, especially if a bra's lining or trim differs from the leggings fabric or if stock came from separate lots. Confirm what counts as an acceptable match for the intended collection, and ask whether the same pairing can be supported on reorders.</p>
<p>Fitclo’s <a href="/ready-to-ship/">ready-stock catalogue</a> is a starting point to identify styles. Send the exact style references and size matrix for a stock check. For a defined pairing assortment with your own labels or packaging, ask about <a href="/private-label/">private label or a custom order</a>. Availability, MOQ basis and packing costs need to be confirmed for the specific style and route.</p>
